---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { photos, ocean } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Personal Purpose', href: '/explore/personal-purpose/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Personal Purpose — what a chart says about vocation"
  description="Can a birth chart reveal your life purpose? What astrology honestly offers about vocation and direction: the Midheaven, the Sun, the nodes — and the limits of all of them."
  path="/explore/personal-purpose/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>Personal purpose and the chart</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        "What am I here to do?" is the question that brings more people to astrology than any
        other. Here's what a chart can honestly offer — and what it can't.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.floatingInTrust.src}
        alt={ocean.floatingInTrust.alt}
        width={ocean.floatingInTrust.width}
        height={ocean.floatingInTrust.height}
        style="object-position: center 22%;"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What the chart offers</h2>
      <p>
        A chart won't print a job title. What it shows is a pattern of signals that, read
        together, sketch the <em>shape</em> of meaningful work for you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The Sun</strong> — what you're here to become; the activity that generates
          vitality rather than consuming it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The <a href="/explore/houses/tenth-house/">Midheaven and tenth house</a></strong> —
          your public direction: the kind of contribution that fits you at full height.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The <a href="/explore/houses/sixth-house/">sixth</a> and
          <a href="/explore/houses/second-house/">second</a> houses</strong> — how you work
          day to day, and which talents can actually support you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The lunar nodes</strong> — read as a growth axis: gifts that come easily
          (South Node) stretching toward development that doesn't (North Node).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Saturn</strong> — the mastery you're building slowly, whether you meant to or
          not. Dread and vocation are neighbors more often than you'd think.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What the chart doesn't do</h2>
      <p>
        It doesn't choose for you, and it doesn't rescue you from choosing. Several livelihoods
        can honor the same chart; the signals describe the <em>qualities</em> your work needs —
        autonomy, depth, audience, service, craft — not the LinkedIn title. Purpose, in my
        experience, is less discovered than built: the chart tells you what materials you're
        holding.
      </p>
      <h2>A word about seasons</h2>
      <p>
        Purpose questions get loud at particular transits — the Saturn returns near 29 and 58
        most famously. If the question has recently become urgent in your life, timing may be part
        of the answer: see <a href="/explore/transits/">transits</a>, or bring the whole question
        to a <a href="/readings/natal/">natal reading</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Bring the big question"
    body="Vocation and direction are natal reading territory — your chart's signals, read together, connected to your actual options."
  />
</BaseLayout>
